--TEST--
PHP 8's `throw` in expression position raises from inside the expression it is written in — `?? throw` and `?: throw` both, with the `never` the branch contributes absorbed by the union so the position's own declared type still holds (`rule:errors/propagation`, `rule:types/grammar`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:errors/throw
class Box {
    // `$v ?? throw …` is the whole reason expression-position `throw`
    // exists: the coalesce's right half cannot complete, so the method
    // returns `string` rather than `string|never`.
    public static function get(?string $v): string {
        return $v ?? throw new LogicError("empty");
    }
}

echo Box::get("here"), "\n";

try {
    echo Box::get(null), "\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "caught ", $e->message, "\n";
}

// The same in a ternary arm, and the same absorption: a `string` binding
// accepts it because the arm that raises never reaches the join.
try {
    string $t = true ? throw new RuntimeError("arm") : "no";
    echo $t, "\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "caught ", $e->message, "\n";
}

// The statement form is unchanged and still ends its block — this line
// proves the expression form left the surrounding one open.
echo "end", "\n";
--EXPECT--
here
caught empty
caught arm
end
